\title{Antichains, Height, and the Strict Growth of\\ Boolean-Lattice Extremal Numbers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{6 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a finite poset $P$, the extremal number $\La(n,P)$ is the largest size of a family of
subsets of $[n]$ containing no weak copy of $P$, and $\La^{*}(n,P)$ is its analogue for
strong (induced) copies. We study the case in which the forbidden poset is itself a Boolean
lattice $B_d$, the family of all subsets of a $d$-element set ordered by inclusion.

Our central result is an \emph{Antichain Augmentation Theorem}: if $\FF \subseteq 2^{[n]}$
contains no weak copy of $B_d$ and $\LL \subseteq 2^{[n]}$ is an antichain, then
$\FF \cup \LL$ contains no weak copy of $B_{d+1}$; the same holds for strong copies. The
proof rests on a lifting construction: for every antichain $A \subseteq B_{d+1}$ there is an
order embedding $B_d \hookrightarrow B_{d+1}$ whose image is disjoint from $A$, obtained by
adjoining the new atom exactly along an up-set determined by $A$.

Three families of consequences follow. First, strict monotonicity in the forbidden
dimension: $\La(n,B_d) < \La(n,B_{d+1})$ and $\La^{*}(n,B_d) < \La^{*}(n,B_{d+1})$ for every
$n \ge d$, the condition $d \le n$ being necessary as well as sufficient, and
$\La(n,B_d) + k \le \La(n,B_{d+k})$ whenever $d+k \le n+1$. Second, the quantitative
refinement $2^{n} + n\,\La(n,B_d) \le (n+1)\,\La(n,B_{d+1})$, valid for all $n$ and $d$, so
that the gain $\La(n,B_{d+1}) - \La(n,B_d)$ is at least $(2^{n} - \La(n,B_d))/(n+1)$, of
order $\central/\sqrt{n}$ for fixed $d$. Third, height criteria: a family with no chain of
$d+1$ sets is weak $B_d$-free, while weak $B_d$-freeness forces the absence of chains of
$2^{d}$ sets; both thresholds are sharp. We complement the theory with exhaustive
computations for $n \le 4$ and a programme of falsifiable conjectures.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The forbidden subposet problem}

Let $[n] = \{1,\dots,n\}$ and let $2^{[n]}$ denote the power set of $[n]$ ordered by
inclusion. Given a finite poset $P$, the forbidden subposet problem asks for the maximum
size of a family $\FF \subseteq 2^{[n]}$ that does not contain $P$ in a prescribed sense.

The subject begins with Sperner's theorem: a family in which no member contains another has
at most $\central$ members, with equality for a central layer. Erd\H{o}s extended this: a
family with no chain of $k+1$ sets has at most the sum of the $k$ largest binomial
coefficients $\binom{n}{i}$, attained by $k$ consecutive central layers. Both results
identify the extremum with a layer family, and much of the field concerns the extent to
which this persists for other forbidden posets.

For a general $P$ the answer is governed, conjecturally, by a layer invariant $e(P)$: the
largest $d$ such that the union of some $d$ consecutive layers is weak $P$-free for all
large $n$. The Boolean lattices $B_d$ are the fundamental test cases, with
$e(B_d) = e^{*}(B_d) = d$; the case $d = 3$ is the first in which the conjectural equality
$\La(n,P) = (e(P)+o(1))\central$ is in doubt.

\subsection{What this paper does}

We study how $\La(n,B_d)$ behaves as $d$ varies with $n$ fixed. Monotonicity,
$\La(n,B_d) \le \La(n,B_{d+1})$, is immediate, since a weak copy of $B_{d+1}$ contains a
weak copy of $B_d$. Strictness is not: the naive attempt---adjoin a missing set to an
extremal family---founders because deleting one vertex from a copy of $B_{d+1}$ leaves no
copy of $B_d$ in any obvious way. We resolve this by means of a lifting construction inside
the cube (Section~\ref{sec:lift}), which yields the Antichain Augmentation Theorem
(Section~\ref{sec:aug}). Everything else is an application: strict monotonicity in full
(Section~\ref{sec:strict}), a pigeonhole strengthening (Section~\ref{sec:quant}), and height
criteria with matching sharpness examples (Section~\ref{sec:height}).
Section~\ref{sec:comp} reports exhaustive computations for $n \le 4$, and
Section~\ref{sec:disc} lays out the resulting conjectures.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\alpha$ is a finite ground set with $|\alpha| = n$, and $B_d$ denotes the
Boolean lattice of all subsets of a fixed $d$-element index set, ordered by inclusion; thus
$|B_d| = 2^{d}$ and $B_1$ is a two-element chain.

\begin{definition}[Weak copy]
A family $\FF \subseteq 2^{\alpha}$ \emph{contains a weak copy} of a finite poset $P$ if
there is an injective map $\iota : P \to \FF$ with
$p < q \Rightarrow \iota(p) \subsetneq \iota(q)$ for all $p,q \in P$. It is
\emph{weak $P$-free} if no such $\iota$ exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Strong copy]
$\FF$ \emph{contains a strong copy} of $P$ if there is an injective $\iota : P \to \FF$ with
$p < q \Leftrightarrow \iota(p) \subsetneq \iota(q)$, so incomparable elements map to
incomparable sets. It is \emph{strong $P$-free} if no such $\iota$ exists.
\end{definition}

Every strong copy is a weak copy, so weak $P$-freeness implies strong $P$-freeness.

\begin{definition}[Extremal numbers]
$\La(n,P)$ is the maximum size of a weak $P$-free family in $2^{[n]}$, and $\La^{*}(n,P)$
the maximum size of a strong $P$-free one. Both maxima are attained, and
$\La(n,P) \le \La^{*}(n,P)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Antichain, chain, height]
$\LL \subseteq 2^{\alpha}$ is an \emph{antichain} if no member is a proper subset of
another. $\FF$ \emph{has a chain of $k$ sets} if $A_1 \subsetneq \cdots \subsetneq A_k$ with
all $A_i \in \FF$; the \emph{height} of $\FF$ is the largest such $k$. The \emph{maximal
sets} $\max\FF$ are the members not properly contained in another member; $\max\FF$ is an
antichain, nonempty when $\FF$ is.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Layers and level families]
$\Lay(a,k) = \{A \subseteq [n] : a \le |A| < a+k\}$ is the union of $k$ consecutive layers;
for $S \subseteq \{0,\dots,n\}$ the \emph{level family} is
$\LL(S) = \{A \subseteq [n] : |A| \in S\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}\label{fact:background}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\La(n,B_1) = \La^{*}(n,B_1) = \central$ (Sperner).
\item $\Lay(a,d)$ is weak $B_d$-free for every $a$, so $\La(n,B_d)$ is at least the sum of
the $d$ largest binomial coefficients $\binom{n}{i}$; moreover this layer family is
maximal: adding any set of size outside $[a,a+d)$ creates a strong copy of $B_d$.
\item Among level families, and more generally among permutation-invariant families, the
$d$ central layers are exactly optimal.
\item $\La(n,B_d) \le (2^{d}-1)\central$, whence
$3\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor - 2} \le \La(n,B_3) \le 7\central$.
\item $\La(d+1,B_d) = \La^{*}(d+1,B_d) = 2^{d+1}-2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{fact}

Item~(3) is why the $B_3$ problem is delicate: any construction beating $3\central$ by a
constant factor must break the symmetry of the cube.

\section{The lifting construction}\label{sec:lift}

Fix $d \ge 0$ and realise $B_{d+1}$ as the power set of $\{1,\dots,d,\star\}$, so that $B_d$
is the power set of $\{1,\dots,d\}$. Write $\kappa : B_d \to B_{d+1}$ for the inclusion
$X \mapsto X$ (the ``bottom face''); then $\star \notin \kappa(X)$ for all $X$, and
$\kappa(X) \subseteq \kappa(Y)$ iff $X \subseteq Y$.

\begin{definition}
$U \subseteq B_d$ is an \emph{up-set} if $X \in U$ and $X \subseteq Y$ imply $Y \in U$. For
an up-set $U$ define $\lambda_U : B_d \to B_{d+1}$ by
\[
\lambda_U(X) =
\begin{cases}
\kappa(X) \cup \{\star\}, & X \in U,\\
\kappa(X), & X \notin U.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Lifting Lemma]\label{lem:lift}
For every up-set $U \subseteq B_d$ and all $X,Y \in B_d$,
$\lambda_U(X) \subseteq \lambda_U(Y) \iff X \subseteq Y$. Consequently $\lambda_U$ is
injective and $\lambda_U(X) \subsetneq \lambda_U(Y) \iff X \subsetneq Y$: it is an order
embedding of $B_d$ into $B_{d+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Four cases.
If $X,Y \in U$, then $\kappa(X)\cup\{\star\} \subseteq \kappa(Y)\cup\{\star\}$ iff
$\kappa(X) \subseteq \kappa(Y)\cup\{\star\}$ iff $\kappa(X) \subseteq \kappa(Y)$ (as
$\star \notin \kappa(X)$) iff $X \subseteq Y$.
If $X,Y \notin U$, this is the corresponding property of $\kappa$.
If $X \notin U$ and $Y \in U$, then $\kappa(X) \subseteq \kappa(Y)\cup\{\star\}$ iff
$\kappa(X) \subseteq \kappa(Y)$ iff $X \subseteq Y$.
If $X \in U$ and $Y \notin U$, the left side fails since $\star \in \lambda_U(X)$ but
$\star \notin \lambda_U(Y)$; the right side fails too, because $X \subseteq Y$ would force
$Y \in U$ as $U$ is an up-set. Injectivity and the strict form follow by antisymmetry.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Avoidance]\label{prop:avoid}
Let $\iota : B_{d+1} \to 2^{\alpha}$ be injective with
$p \subsetneq q \Rightarrow \iota(p) \subsetneq \iota(q)$, and let $\LL \subseteq 2^{\alpha}$
be an antichain. Put
\[
U = \{X \in B_d : \exists\, Z \subseteq X \text{ with } \iota(\kappa(Z)) \in \LL\}.
\]
Then $U$ is an up-set and $\iota(\lambda_U(X)) \notin \LL$ for every $X \in B_d$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$U$ is an up-set: a witness $Z$ for $X$ is a witness for any $Y \supseteq X$.

If $X \notin U$, then $\lambda_U(X) = \kappa(X)$, and $\iota(\kappa(X)) \in \LL$ would
witness $X \in U$ with $Z = X$; so $\iota(\lambda_U(X)) \notin \LL$.

If $X \in U$, pick $Z \subseteq X$ with $\iota(\kappa(Z)) \in \LL$. Then
$\kappa(Z) \subseteq \kappa(X) \subsetneq \kappa(X)\cup\{\star\} = \lambda_U(X)$, so
$\iota(\kappa(Z)) \subsetneq \iota(\lambda_U(X))$. Were $\iota(\lambda_U(X)) \in \LL$, the
antichain $\LL$ would contain two distinct comparable members, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:embedavoid}
For every antichain $A \subseteq B_{d+1}$ there is an order embedding
$\varphi : B_d \hookrightarrow B_{d+1}$ with $\varphi(B_d) \cap A = \emptyset$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The Antichain Augmentation Theorem}\label{sec:aug}

\begin{theorem}[Antichain Augmentation, weak form]\label{thm:augweak}
If $\FF \subseteq 2^{\alpha}$ is weak $B_d$-free and $\LL \subseteq 2^{\alpha}$ is an
antichain, then $\FF \cup \LL$ is weak $B_{d+1}$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\iota : B_{d+1} \to \FF \cup \LL$ is injective with
$p \subsetneq q \Rightarrow \iota(p) \subsetneq \iota(q)$. Let $U$ be as in
Proposition~\ref{prop:avoid} and set $\psi = \iota \circ \lambda_U$. Then $\psi$ is
injective as a composition of injections, and $X \subsetneq Y$ implies
$\lambda_U(X) \subsetneq \lambda_U(Y)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:lift}, hence
$\psi(X) \subsetneq \psi(Y)$. Each value $\psi(X)$ lies in $\FF \cup \LL$ but not in $\LL$,
hence in $\FF$. So $\psi$ is a weak copy of $B_d$ inside $\FF$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Antichain Augmentation, strong form]\label{thm:augstrong}
If $\FF$ is strong $B_d$-free and $\LL$ is an antichain, then $\FF \cup \LL$ is strong
$B_{d+1}$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A strong copy $\iota$ of $B_{d+1}$ in $\FF \cup \LL$ satisfies in particular
$p \subsetneq q \Rightarrow \iota(p) \subsetneq \iota(q)$, so Proposition~\ref{prop:avoid}
applies and $\psi = \iota \circ \lambda_U$ maps into $\FF$. Combining the equivalence of
Lemma~\ref{lem:lift} with that defining $\iota$,
\[
X \subsetneq Y \iff \lambda_U(X) \subsetneq \lambda_U(Y) \iff \psi(X) \subsetneq \psi(Y),
\]
so $\psi$ is a strong copy of $B_d$ inside $\FF$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

No hypothesis relates $\FF$ and $\LL$: they may overlap arbitrarily. The theorem is
efficient in a precise sense: an antichain may have $\central$ members---an exponentially
large addition---yet the forbidden dimension rises by exactly one. Since a family is an
antichain precisely when it is weak $B_1$-free, Theorem~\ref{thm:augweak} is the case
$e = 1$ of Conjecture~\ref{conj:F1} below.

\section{Strict monotonicity}\label{sec:strict}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:notall}
If $d \le n$ then $2^{[n]}$ contains a strong copy of $B_d$, so
$\La(n,B_d) < 2^{n}$ and $\La^{*}(n,B_d) < 2^{n}$. If $d > n$ then
$\La(n,B_d) = \La^{*}(n,B_d) = 2^{n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $d \le n$ fix a $d$-element $D \subseteq [n]$; the identity on $2^{D}$ is a strong copy
of $B_d$, so the full power set is not free. For $d > n$ a copy of $B_d$ needs
$2^{d} > 2^{n}$ distinct sets, which $2^{[n]}$ does not have.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]\label{thm:strict}
For every $n$ and every $d \le n$,
\[
\La(n,B_d) < \La(n,B_{d+1}), \qquad \La^{*}(n,B_d) < \La^{*}(n,B_{d+1}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\FF$ be extremal weak $B_d$-free, so $|\FF| = \La(n,B_d) < 2^{n}$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:notall}; pick $A \notin \FF$. The singleton $\{A\}$ is an antichain, so
$\FF \cup \{A\}$ is weak $B_{d+1}$-free by Theorem~\ref{thm:augweak} and has
$\La(n,B_d)+1$ members. The strong case is identical via Theorem~\ref{thm:augstrong}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact criterion]\label{thm:iff}
$\La(n,B_d) < \La(n,B_{d+1})$ holds if and only if $d \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Sufficiency is Theorem~\ref{thm:strict}. If $d > n$ then also $d + 1 > n$, so both sides
equal $2^{n}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:notall}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:iterated}
If $d + k \le n+1$ then $\La(n,B_d) + k \le \La(n,B_{d+k})$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$, the step being Theorem~\ref{thm:strict} applied at $d+m \le n$.
\end{proof}

In particular $\La(n,B_2) < \La(n,B_3)$ for all $n \ge 2$ and $\La(n,B_3) < \La(n,B_4)$ for
all $n \ge 3$: the cube posets around the open case are strictly separated at every
ground-set size, not merely asymptotically.

\section{A pigeonhole refinement}\label{sec:quant}

\begin{lemma}[A large antichain outside a family]\label{lem:bigantichain}
For every $\FF \subseteq 2^{[n]}$ there is an antichain $\LL$ disjoint from $\FF$ with
$2^{n} \le |\FF| + (n+1)|\LL|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $\CC = 2^{[n]} \setminus \FF$ and partition it into the $n+1$ size classes
$\CC_i = \{A \in \CC : |A| = i\}$. Choose $i_0$ maximising $|\CC_{i_0}|$ and let
$\LL = \CC_{i_0}$; distinct sets of equal size are incomparable, so $\LL$ is an antichain
disjoint from $\FF$, and
$2^{n} - |\FF| = \sum_{i=0}^{n} |\CC_i| \le (n+1)|\CC_{i_0}|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative strict monotonicity]\label{thm:pigeon}
For all $n \ge 0$ and $d \ge 0$,
\[
2^{n} + n\,\La(n,B_d) \;\le\; (n+1)\,\La(n,B_{d+1}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\FF$ be extremal weak $B_d$-free and $\LL$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:bigantichain}. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:augweak}, $\FF \cup \LL$ is weak $B_{d+1}$-free, and by disjointness
$|\FF| + |\LL| \le \La(n,B_{d+1})$. Hence
\[
(n+1)\La(n,B_{d+1}) \ \ge\ (n+1)|\FF| + (n+1)|\LL| \ \ge\ (n+1)|\FF| + 2^{n} - |\FF|
\ =\ 2^{n} + n\,\La(n,B_d). \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:gain}
$\La(n,B_{d+1}) - \La(n,B_d) \ge \dfrac{2^{n} - \La(n,B_d)}{n+1}$, and unconditionally
$(n+1)\La(n,B_{d+1}) \ge 2^{n}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Size of the gain]\label{rem:gain}
Fix $d$ and let $n \to \infty$. By Fact~\ref{fact:background}(4),
$\La(n,B_d) = O_d(2^{n}/\sqrt{n}) = o(2^{n})$, so Corollary~\ref{cor:gain} gives
\[
\La(n,B_{d+1}) - \La(n,B_d) \ \ge\ (1-o(1))\frac{2^{n}}{n+1}
\ =\ \Theta\!\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\central\right).
\]
The natural conjecture is a gain of a full $\central$---as holds for the level-restricted
problem, where the optima differ by exactly one central binomial coefficient---so the
pigeonhole bound falls short by a factor $\Theta(\sqrt{n})$. The loss is easy to localise:
Lemma~\ref{lem:bigantichain} charges the complement to $n+1$ layers uniformly, whereas the
complement of a near-extremal family is concentrated away from the middle.
\end{remark}

\section{Height criteria and their sharpness}\label{sec:height}

\begin{lemma}[Peeling]\label{lem:peel}
For every nonempty family $\FF$, the set $\max\FF$ is a nonempty antichain, and if
$\FF \setminus \max\FF$ has a chain of $k+1$ sets then $\FF$ has a chain of $k+2$ sets.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Finiteness gives that every member is contained in a maximal one, so $\max\FF \ne \emptyset$,
and by definition no member of $\max\FF$ properly contains another. Given a chain
$A_1 \subsetneq \cdots \subsetneq A_{k+1}$ in $\FF \setminus \max\FF$, the set $A_{k+1}$ is
not maximal in $\FF$, hence is properly contained in some $B \in \max\FF$, extending the
chain.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Height Criterion]\label{thm:height}
If $\FF \subseteq 2^{\alpha}$ has no chain of $d+1$ sets, then $\FF$ is weak $B_d$-free, and
hence strong $B_d$-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $d$. For $d=0$, no chain of one set means $\FF = \emptyset$, which contains no
copy of the one-element poset $B_0$. For the step, if $\FF$ has no chain of $d+2$ sets then
$\FF \setminus \max\FF$ has no chain of $d+1$ sets by Lemma~\ref{lem:peel}, so it is weak
$B_d$-free by induction; as $\max\FF$ is an antichain, Theorem~\ref{thm:augweak} makes
$(\FF \setminus \max\FF) \cup \max\FF = \FF$ weak $B_{d+1}$-free.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:height} subsumes the classical construction, since $\Lay(a,d)$ has no chain
of $d+1$ sets; but it applies to arbitrary bounded-height families, complete or not,
symmetric or not.

\begin{corollary}[Few sizes]\label{cor:fewsizes}
If the members of $\FF$ realise at most $d$ distinct cardinalities, then $\FF$ is weak
$B_d$-free.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A chain of $d+1$ sets has $d+1$ distinct cardinalities; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:height}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:chainbound}
If $\FF$ is weak $B_d$-free then $\FF$ has no chain of $2^{d}$ sets.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Given a chain $A_1 \subsetneq \cdots \subsetneq A_{2^{d}}$ in $\FF$, enumerate $B_d$ as
$p_1,\dots,p_{2^{d}}$ in an order refining its partial order. Then $p_i \mapsto A_i$ is
injective and $p_i < p_j$ implies $i < j$, hence $A_i \subsetneq A_j$: a weak copy of $B_d$.
\end{proof}

Thus weak $B_d$-freeness is sandwiched between two height conditions,
\[
\text{height} \le d \implies \text{weak } B_d\text{-free} \implies \text{height} \le 2^{d}-1,
\]
and both implications are optimal.

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness of the lower threshold]
For every $d \le n$ there is a family $\FF \subseteq 2^{[n]}$ with no chain of $d+2$ sets
that contains a strong copy of $B_d$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Take $\FF = 2^{D}$ for a $d$-element $D \subseteq [n]$: its members have only the $d+1$
sizes $0,\dots,d$, and it is literally a copy of $B_d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness of the upper threshold]
If $2^{d} \le n+2$ there is a weak $B_d$-free family in $2^{[n]}$ containing a chain of
$2^{d}-1$ sets.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since $2^{d}-1 \le n+1$, a chain $A_1 \subsetneq \cdots \subsetneq A_{2^{d}-1}$ of subsets
of $[n]$ exists. It has fewer than $2^{d}$ members, and a copy of $B_d$ needs $2^{d}$
distinct sets.
\end{proof}

Between the thresholds---height between $d+1$ and $2^{d}-1$---freeness is not determined by
height alone, and this is exactly where the difficulty of the $B_3$ problem lives.

